# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the tp4/s6 window (the ship-6 candidate; scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it): copy it over
# .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of tp4/s6 and push a tag experiment/c2-serving-vN, ONE TAG AT A TIME (wait for the run to finish; never cancel a running card job).
# Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are resolved at run time. The jobs are templates only: no tag is pushed from the branch by its author. ORDER.txt and
# ORDER-SHIP.txt have the order, the stop rules, the minutes and the read rules of the two runs.
# ONE IMAGE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW: C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-s6-1 is a PLACEHOLDER for the tag B0 builds from the pushed branch head WITH the K64j-OQ graft (sed -i 's/tp4-s6-1/<tag>/' *.env). A commit of a file
# the image carries needs a new B0 build and a new tag in every template. This window never stops, starts or hands back the node agent.
# C2_MODEL_REPO / C2_MODEL_REVISION (qwen_c2_checkpoint.py): a job that carries them serves the Swift checkpoint (what production serves) from its own converted-weight directory; the keys are the same in every
# smoke job of this pack, so each pair differs by the profile alone. The replay and build steps do not forward the selection (the job reader refuses the keys beside them): they run the base checkpoint.
# T1A (TIMED, the CONTROL of the T1 pair): the profile that serves production (ship 5), the CONTROL of the pair c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s5
# on Swift. Tests: np1_live1, np1_live2 and np1_live4 (one, two and four users started together on ~3,500-token real-code prompts, every answer to EXACTLY 2,000 tokens with
# ignore_eos: the live counts WC acts at two and four of, and the lone user NP1 serves); prefill_few_decoders (the governor's shape); concurrent8_steady (eight users: the
# octo block, which neither WC nor the governor touches, so a parity row and the boot-to-boot floor); agent_turns last (the main judge). prefill_few_decoders (ONE decoder
# and a ~120k cold arrival, then TWO decoders and a ~254k one): per shape the arrival's first-token time and prefill rate, each decoder's longest gap from the arrival on
# and its progress inside the arrival's window. ESTIMATE (docs/lever-n-adaptive-governor.md, from the timing model, never measured at 254k): the candidate's first token
# comes 31 to 44 percent sooner (254k: about 182-187 s to 114-119 s; 120k: about 85 s to 50 s) and the decoders get about a fifth of the control's rate inside that window.
# Read the pair with `python3 scripts/ci/prefill_ladder_report.py <T1A smoke log> --second <T1B smoke log>`; the answers of the cold arrivals must hash EQUAL between the
# arms (greedy text, only WHEN the rounds run moves). agent_turns (6 sessions of 64k, 128k and 200k tokens, 12 returning turns each) is the MAIN JUDGE: the ship-5 window's
# T1B number (50.36 tok/s aggregate, returning turn first token p50 1.46 s, p90 2.33 s) is its baseline. agent_turns8 is EIGHT sessions weighted like production traffic
# (contexts of 24k to 80k tokens and one of ~200k, about 550k tokens in all, mean ~69k against production's ~59k; answers capped at 300 to 1,080 tokens, mean 690 like
# production's; the same gaps and 12 turns), every seat busy, no new turn after 25 minutes, an estimate of 15 minutes: the production load, where WC (2 or 4 users) and the
# governor (1 or 2 decoders) mostly sit out, so the pair says whether anything is LOST there. Compare it with its own control leg, never with agent_turns. Read both legs
# with `python3 scripts/ci/agent_turns.py report --smoke-log <smoke> --container-log <container> --test <name>`: the rates, the TTFT by context bucket, the tokens
# re-prefilled, the per-live table; the per-turn answer hashes must be EQUAL between the legs. Every boot a fresh reset. READ: c2_smoke_check clean on both legs; then
# python3 scripts/ci/np1_report.py --control-a <T1A smoke> --arm-a <T1B smoke> --control-b <T1A smoke> --arm-b <T1B smoke> --arm-container <T1B container> --profile
# c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s6 (an ABAB reader fed one boot a side: each smoke is passed twice, the boot-to-boot floors read exactly 1; a
# LOSS of 2 to 5 percent at a parity row is boot noise until a second pair says otherwise) and python3 scripts/ci/octo_compare.py --control <T1A smoke> --octo <T1B smoke>
# --octo-container <T1B container> --profile c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s6 (every answer equal, user by user). The control logs none of WC's
# or the governor's lines (the smoke check fails a line on a profile that does not ask for it). RUN WITH THE CI PAUSED: the ARC runner sets are at ZERO for this job (ORDER,
# ARC-ZERO); no build, no CI push, no other job beside it. CHECKPOINT READ (the workflow passes c2_smoke_check.py --expect-checkpoint): the container log holds "[QWEN-C2]
# checkpoint ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b@b4c84d42903a8646b25857eb2827288d92ed85a4 key=... weight_cache=.../weights-ckpt/<key> base_cache=... (the base cache is not read or
# written)" once, a "weight cache ...: found" line (the cache the Swift window built; this pack never builds it) and the worker's "[QWEN-C2] checkpoint bound ..." line; the
# models answer names ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b. TELEMETRY (C2_TELEMETRY=1, scripts/ci/card_telemetry.py): the read-only ARC sidecar samples every chip once a second
# through the smoke step; the first [TELEMETRY] line must say decode check ok. A rate difference that comes with a different loaded AICLK between the arms is the clocks,
# not the lever. Duration (estimate): 60 minutes.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset smoke
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-s6-1
C2_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s5
C2_MODEL_REPO=ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b
C2_MODEL_REVISION=b4c84d42903a8646b25857eb2827288d92ed85a4
C2_SMOKE_TESTS=warmup,np1_live1,np1_live2,np1_live4,prefill_few_decoders,concurrent8_steady,agent_turns
C2_TELEMETRY=1
C2_BOX_MINUTES=115
